## 1. Setup

Before we begin, make sure to have the following prerequisites met:

- NodeJS and npm installed on your machine
- A Discord application created from the [Discord Developer Portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications)
- A Guild or Server where you want your Discord Bot to be hosted.

Once done, install the necessary dependencies by running this command in your terminal:

```
npm install discord.js@latest dotenv
```

Create a new `.env` file at the root of your project directory and add your `DISCORD_TOKEN` (generated after creating an Application in Discord Developer Portal). 

Your `.env` should contain the following lines:
```dotenv
DISCORD_TOKEN=<your-discord-token-here>
```

Next, initialize a new TypeScript project with the name "discord-bot" using:

```
npx yoast tsconfig-json
cd discoboT

# Rename.example.json files to.json
cp./tsconfig.example.json tsconfig.json
cp./package.json.example package.json

# Create src folder
mkdir src
```


## 2. Initialize Discord.js with Intents

Inside your `src/index.ts`, import necessary modules & interfaces:

```TypeScript
import { Client, GatewayIntentBits, PermissionsBitField } from 'discord.js';
import { Config } from './Config';

const client = new Client({
    intents: [
        GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
        GatewayIntentBits.GuildBans,
        GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
        GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent
    ],
});

// Interface for your configuration
interface Config {
    token: string;
}

// This interface defines Command Permission Levels
type CommandPermissions = Record<string, PermissionsBitField>;
```

Add this function named `loadCommands()` which will load all available commands. This function can go anywhere in your index.ts file.

```TypeScript
async function loadCommands() {
    const commandFiles = await fs.readdirSync('./commands');   
    for (const file of commandFiles) {        
        const command = await import(`./commands/${file}`);
        if (!command.default) continue;

        command.default.client = client; // Pass the client reference to each command (command needs this!)
        client.commands.set(command.default.name, command); // Add it to the client instance so that other parts of our code can rely on its presence.
    }
}
```

**Note:** Ensure that you have structured your commands folder properly - Each `.cmd.ts` command file represents a separate command.


## 3. Define Your Configuration

Create a new `Config.ts` file inside your `src` folder, define your configurations as follows:

```TypeScript
export default {
    token: process.env.DISCORD_TOKEN,
};
```

## 4. Set up Event Handlers

Add the event handler for `ready` event, which gets triggered when the Discord bot is ready to receive messages:

```TypeScript
client.on("ready", () => {
    console.log(`Logged in as ${client.user.username}!`);
});

// Load Commands
(async () => {
    await loadCommands();
})();
```

## 5. Implement Slash Commands

Import required types from `discord.js` and set up your `GuildApplicationCommandManager`.

```TypeScript
import { REST } from '@discordjs/rest';
importchalk from 'chalk';
import * as fs from 'fs';

// Importing command(s)
import { BanCommand } from "./commands/ban.cmd";

// Define your Command Manager
const rest = new REST({ version: '10' }).setToken(client.config.token);
```

Define your Command Collections:

```TypeScript
